                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
                        "entry_id": "b91e8fcf-bcab-315e-b1e3-e9681c060a0a",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Interprète de la loi musulmane."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6dbabab4-dc76-4411-bede-8580e0b12fa8",
                                "sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
                                "text": "La charge de cadis lesker est mise chez les Turcs au rang des dignités ecclésiastiques. […] Le cadis lesker est regardé comme le lieutenant du mufti."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e8dbade1-3619-41a1-8ef6-7e5665fe3e0a",
                                "sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
                                "text": "Lorsqu’il s’agit de délibérations importantes, ils [les vizirs] sont admis dans le conseil du cabinet avec le grand-vizir, le mufti & les cadileskers. Ce sont eux qui écrivent ordinairement le nom du grand-seigneur au haut de ses ordonnances, & le sultan pour les autoriser, fait apposer son sceau au-dessous de son nom."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cc02304c-282f-459c-bb30-adc032dcb963",
                                "sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
                                "text": "La réponse du mufti doit commencer par la formule liminaire religieuse courante, la basmala, suivie d’une formule d’action de grâces. S’il s’agit d’une fatwa demandée par le Prince, le mufti ajoutera à la basmala une prière attirant les grâces du ciel sur le consultant."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fe83b782-5b52-4ef3-9bc0-af7b56999207",
                        "source_entry_id": "b91e8fcf-bcab-315e-b1e3-e9681c060a0a",
                        "source_sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
                        "target_lang_word": "Mufti",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Mufti",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4f11f2ea-45a5-303b-8323-3201d4329770",
                                        "entry_id": "2df1453a-e5af-3fd6-8e2a-8e4be0f7d736",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "islamischer Rechtsgelehrter, offizieller Gesetzesausleger, der islamrechtliche Gutachten (Fatwas/Fetwas) erstellt"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Islam"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e2b9f89e-f3d0-41ec-aa03-c0971b1ba589",
                                                "sense_id": "4f11f2ea-45a5-303b-8323-3201d4329770",
                                                "text": "„Der saudische Mufti Scheich Abdul Aziz al Scheich erklärte, das Töten Unschuldiger sei im Islam verboten.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "c9d32a47-03ed-4955-99b1-908fa161de25",
                        "source_entry_id": "b91e8fcf-bcab-315e-b1e3-e9681c060a0a",
                        "source_sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
                        "target_lang_word": "mufti",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "mufti",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c1425751-3f91-3514-a8ba-46d4c3ba3cfc",
                                        "entry_id": "c858f027-2562-3eea-bf56-66199bd28b0d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A Muslim scholar and interpreter of sharia law, who can deliver a fatwa."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c8127eac-f683-44ef-83fa-81e59cdc74cd",
                                                "sense_id": "c1425751-3f91-3514-a8ba-46d4c3ba3cfc",
                                                "text": "Mujtahidd's online claims have prompted an aggressive backlash against social media from the Saudi religious establishment. The grand mufti of Saudi Arabia, Sheikh Abdul-Aziz al-Sheikh, said in January that Twitter was a platform for \"promoting lies\" and a \"dangerous practice\" that should be avoided by Muslims. Commentators have described the phenomenon as symbolic of the growing political debate about use of Twitter in Saudi Arabia."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2d713ada-33e6-38a6-8fee-7388c47efaf4",
                                        "entry_id": "c858f027-2562-3eea-bf56-66199bd28b0d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A civilian dress when worn by a member of the military, or casual dress when worn by a pupil of a school who normally would wear uniform."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia",
                                            "British",
                                            "New-Zealand",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0b36decc-60c5-4bad-982b-e4433e7b70fc",
                                                "sense_id": "2d713ada-33e6-38a6-8fee-7388c47efaf4",
                                                "text": "He had a suit of summer mufti, and a broad-brimmed blue beaver hat looped with leaves broken from the hedgerows in the lanes, and a Leander scarf tucked full of flowers: loosestrife, meadowrue, orchis, ragged-robin."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8a8b8b1f-37ff-48a6-948e-365751045c5b",
                                                "sense_id": "2d713ada-33e6-38a6-8fee-7388c47efaf4",
                                                "text": "The innate reluctance of the Englishman to make himself conspicuous has stood him here in good stead. Except on special occasions, the British officers are almost always in mufti."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f0049ebb-b85c-4d83-80b6-b279be5626d6",
                                                "sense_id": "2d713ada-33e6-38a6-8fee-7388c47efaf4",
                                                "text": "The neutrality, however, causes some peculiar situations, such as that arising from the prohibition of uniforms other than those of the Eireann Defence Forces and the Diplomatic Corps. This would be simple if no one from Eire were a sailor, soldier, or airman in the British Forces, but thousands of them are, and as such they may not wear mufti unless on leave from the B.E.F."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f913194c-dc57-4ca7-bb9c-7f3ea810f743",
                                                "sense_id": "2d713ada-33e6-38a6-8fee-7388c47efaf4",
                                                "text": "The sailors in mufti returned with 140 quarts of Bar's Leak, half of which was poured into the condenser."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "4f3c92e6-f6b2-4903-ac68-15fb2dc7ef12",
                        "source_entry_id": "b91e8fcf-bcab-315e-b1e3-e9681c060a0a",
                        "source_sense_id": "dbe69446-c63e-3d4a-96f8-23a2b6a16ed8",
